misc: decd: add suppress_vsync_sync for MIPS-alive testing

EXPERIMENT, out of series.  With the MIPS window layer alive a real frame
submit hard-locks the SoC while decd-client blue does not.  This gates the
vsync handler's four-slot ring rewrite so the fault can be localised.

Result 2026-09-04: with suppress_vsync_sync=1 the submit completes and the
board survives -- but the ring registers stay zero, so no frame is delivered.
It localises the lock to dec_frame_queue_sync's register writes and rules out
the ioctl, the dma-buf import, the IOMMU mapping and PM.  It is NOT a safe
submit path.

--- a/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_frame.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/sunxi_decd/decd_frame.c
@@
+/*
+ * Experiment 2026-09-04: with the MIPS window layer ALIVE a real frame submit
+ * hard-locks the SoC, while decd-client blue does not.  The difference is this
+ * path -- our vsync handler owns SPI 142 at ~60 Hz and rewrites all four ring
+ * slots (Y/C/VideoInfo) plus int_to_display on every dirty frame, while the
+ * firmware's NRWinNode slot 4 programs the same source.  suppress_vsync_sync=1
+ * enqueues frames but leaves the ring alone, isolating that rewrite.
+ */
+static bool suppress_vsync_sync;
+module_param(suppress_vsync_sync, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(suppress_vsync_sync,
+		 "do not rewrite the ring from the vsync handler (MIPS-alive test)");
+
 static void dec_vsync_process(struct dec_frame_manager *fmgr)
 {
 	struct dec_frame_queue *q = fmgr->queue;
 	unsigned long flags;
 	int state;
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&q->lock, flags);
 	state = fmgr->state;
+	if (suppress_vsync_sync) {
+		q->dirty = 0;
+		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&q->lock, flags);
+		fmgr->irq_count++;
+		return;
+	}
 	if (q->dirty) {
